window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1957/483":{"facts":"California's constitution, as amended in 1952, denied any tax exemption to a person who advocates the violent overthrow of the federal or state government or support of a foreign government against the United States in wartime. To enforce it, the legislature required anyone claiming a property-tax exemption to sign a declaration on the tax form that he did not engage in that advocacy. The appellants, honorably discharged World War II veterans entitled to California's veterans' property-tax exemption, refused to sign the oath, struck it from their forms, and were denied the exemption solely for that refusal.","procedure":"One veteran sued for declaratory relief and won in the trial court; the other sued to recover taxes paid under protest and lost. The California Supreme Court upheld the provisions in both cases, construing them to reach only speech that could constitutionally be punished as a crime. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction of the appeals, decided together with Prince v. City and County of San Francisco.","question":"May a State that denies a tax exemption to those who engage in unlawful advocacy enforce the denial through a procedure that places on the taxpayer the burden of proving that he does not engage in such speech?","holding":"No. Assuming without deciding that California may deny tax exemptions to people who engage in criminally punishable advocacy, enforcing that rule through procedures that place the burdens of proof and persuasion on the taxpayer denies freedom of speech without the procedural safeguards the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment requires. The State must bear the burden of showing that a claimant engaged in criminal speech.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan began from the premise that “It cannot be gainsaid that a discriminatory denial of a tax exemption for engaging in speech is a limitation on free speech”: denying the exemption penalizes the speech as surely as a fine would, and calling the exemption a privilege does not change that. Because the line between protected and punishable speech is finely drawn, the procedures used to find the facts matter as much as the substantive rule. Under California's scheme the taxpayer bore the burden of producing evidence and of persuading the assessor and the courts that he was not a proscribed advocate, a negative that is hard to prove. A speaker who must prove the lawfulness of his speech will steer far wider of the unlawful zone, so the procedure deters protected speech the State could not forbid directly; “The separation of legitimate from illegitimate speech calls for more sensitive tools than California has supplied.” The Court distinguished the loyalty oaths upheld for public employees, candidates and union officers: those laws aimed at protecting particular positions from misuse rather than at speech itself, and the oaths there were conclusive once signed, not a step in a process that kept the burden of proof on the signer. Because the entire procedure was invalid, the veterans did not have to take its first step by signing the declaration.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, concurred, writing that “California, in effect, has imposed a tax on belief and expression” and that this violates the First Amendment outright, apart from any procedural defect. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Black, concurred, arguing that loyalty oaths invert the presumption of innocence (drawing on Alexander Hamilton and Cummings v. Missouri) and that advocacy not tied to action is protected. Justice Clark dissented: he read California law as making the filed oath conclusive, so that the State, not the taxpayer, bore the risk of a false oath through perjury prosecution, and he would have held that due process does not require the State to bear the burden of proof in a tax proceeding involving no fine or imprisonment. Justice Burton concurred in the result; Chief Justice Warren took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Clark dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep357/usrep357513/usrep357513.pdf"},"1958/66":{"facts":"Respondents ran a lumber and materials business in California. Unions sought an agreement requiring respondents to keep only union members or those who joined within thirty days, and when respondents refused, the unions peacefully picketed the business and pressed customers and suppliers to stop dealing with it. The trial court found that the sole purpose was to compel the agreement, though none of the unions had been designated as the employees' bargaining agent.","procedure":"The state trial court enjoined the picketing and awarded $1,000 in damages. The National Labor Relations Board's regional director had declined jurisdiction. After the Supreme Court held in Guss v. Utah Labor Relations Board that the Board's refusal to act does not return power to the States, it remanded this case on the damages question; the California Supreme Court then set aside the injunction but upheld the damages under state tort and labor law, and the Supreme Court again granted certiorari.","question":"Does the National Labor Relations Act preclude a state court from awarding damages under state law for peaceful union picketing that is arguably protected or prohibited by sections 7 or 8 of the Act, when the Board has declined to exercise jurisdiction?","holding":"Yes. When an activity is arguably subject to section 7 or section 8 of the Act, States as well as federal courts must defer to the exclusive competence of the National Labor Relations Board. The Board's refusal to assert jurisdiction, and the form of the remedy as damages rather than an injunction, do not restore state power.","reasoning":"Justice Frankfurter explained that Congress committed the administration of national labor policy to a centralized expert agency in order to secure uniformity and to avoid conflicts among tribunals, procedures and substantive rules, and that “Administration is more than a means of regulation; administration is regulation.” Conflict with a federal scheme exists whatever form of state law is used, and courts are not the primary tribunals to decide whether conduct is protected or prohibited. Where the Board has not decided the status of conduct that is arguably within sections 7 or 8, state jurisdiction is displaced. Leaving the States free after the Board declines jurisdiction would allow regulation Congress had withdrawn, and damages can regulate conduct as effectively as an injunction, especially since the remedy would be one the Board itself could not give. The Court distinguished Laburnum and Russell, which allowed state damages for violence and threats of violence because of the State's compelling interest in preserving public order, an interest not present here.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Clark, Whittaker and Stewart, concurred in the result only: the picketing could fairly be considered federally protected, so state action was precluded until the Board decided otherwise. He disagreed with the Court's broader rationale, arguing that Laburnum and Russell allowed state damages for nonviolent as well as violent torts that are merely federally prohibited, that the Court's rule leaves injured parties without remedy when the Board declines jurisdiction or delays, and that state power should survive where conduct is neither protected nor prohibited.","disposition":"The judgment of the California Supreme Court was reversed. Justice Frankfurter wrote the opinion of the Court; Justices Harlan, Clark, Whittaker and Stewart concurred in the result.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep359/usrep359236/usrep359236.pdf"},"1957/46":{"facts":"Green was indicted in the District of Columbia on an arson count and a count of first degree (felony) murder for a death caused by the fire. The judge told the jury it could convict him of first or second degree murder on the second count. The jury found him guilty of arson and second degree murder, and its verdict was silent on first degree murder. The judge accepted the verdict, discharged the jury, and sentenced him to five to twenty years for second degree murder.","procedure":"On Green's appeal, the Court of Appeals reversed the second degree murder conviction as unsupported by evidence and remanded for a new trial. On remand Green was tried again for first degree murder under the original indictment, over his plea of former jeopardy, and was convicted and sentenced to death. The en banc Court of Appeals rejected the double jeopardy defense, relying on Trono v. United States, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Fifth Amendment bar retrying a defendant for first degree murder after he obtained reversal of a conviction for second degree murder, when his first jury was authorized to convict of first degree murder but returned a verdict silent on it?","holding":"Yes. The second trial for first degree murder placed Green in jeopardy twice for the same offense. His jeopardy on that charge ended when the first jury was discharged without convicting him of it, and he did not waive his former-jeopardy defense by appealing the second degree conviction.","reasoning":"Justice Black explained that the double jeopardy guarantee protects against repeated attempts by the State to convict, with the anxiety, expense and risk of erroneous conviction that follow. Green was in direct peril of conviction for first degree murder at his first trial and was forced to “run the gantlet” on that charge; the jury had full opportunity to convict and chose the lesser offense, which most courts treat as an implicit acquittal. Even apart from that, the jury was discharged without Green's consent and without extraordinary circumstances, so his jeopardy for first degree murder ended. The Court rejected the claim that Green waived the defense by appealing, since waiver means a knowing and voluntary relinquishment and a man with a long sentence for the lesser crime has no meaningful choice. It also rejected the theory of continuing jeopardy, since first degree murder was not the offense of which he was convicted or that was involved in his appeal, and a defendant cannot be made to surrender protection against a capital prosecution as the price of appealing a different conviction. Trono, a case from the Philippines under a statute and peculiar local procedures with no majority rationale, was not extended to control.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter, joined by Justices Burton, Clark and Harlan, dissented: the common-law and constitutional history, Justice Story and Justice McLean's decisions, United States v. Ball and Trono show that retrial after a defendant's successful appeal is permitted, including for the greater offense, the jury's silence should not be treated as an acquittal, the verdict was likely a compromise caused by the erroneous instruction, the decision effectively overrules Trono and ignores stare decisis, and it turns the clause into an absolute rule disregarding society's interest in a trial free of error.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed. Justice Black wrote the opinion of the Court, announced by Justice Douglas; Justices Frankfurter, Burton, Clark and Harlan dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep355/usrep355184/usrep355184.pdf"},"1959/9":{"facts":"Smith owned a bookstore in Los Angeles. A city ordinance made it unlawful to possess any obscene or indecent writing or book in a place of business where books are sold. The state courts read the ordinance as imposing absolute liability: Smith was convicted and jailed solely because an obscene book was found in his store, with no element of knowledge of its contents.","procedure":"The municipal court convicted Smith, and the Appellate Department of the Superior Court affirmed. He appealed to the Supreme Court, arguing the ordinance, as construed, violated the Constitution, and the Court assumed without deciding that the book was obscene.","question":"Does a city ordinance that makes a bookseller criminally liable for possessing an obscene book, without requiring that he know its contents, violate the freedom of the press protected by the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. Eliminating any knowledge requirement tends to restrict the public's access to constitutionally protected books, so the ordinance, as construed and applied, violates the freedom of the press safeguarded by the Fourteenth Amendment. The Court did not decide what mental element would be constitutionally sufficient.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan noted that books and their distribution, even for commercial gain, are within the freedom of the press, and that booksellers play a significant role in distribution. States may generally create strict-liability offenses, but doing so here works a substantial restriction on expression, and legal devices valid in most settings cannot be applied where they inhibit expression, as in Speiser v. Randall. Although obscene speech is unprotected under Roth, the ordinance's strict liability would penalize booksellers with no notice of a book's character, and a bookseller could not read every book in stock, so he would restrict his stock to books he has inspected. That self-censorship, compelled by the State, would reduce access to protected as well as obscene books and affect the whole public. The analogy to strict liability for food and drug distributors fails because no specific constitutional protection attaches to food sales. The argument that proving knowledge is too hard was rejected since knowledge can be inferred from circumstances. The Court left open what mental element suffices, whether honest mistake excuses, and whether a bookseller can be required to investigate.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment but not the reasoning, arguing that punishing possession of obscene books burdens the press whether or not knowledge is required and that the First Amendment's \"no law\" language leaves no room for balancing it against other interests. Justice Frankfurter concurred, saying the Court should have indicated the required scope of scienter and that the Court's decision should not nullify the State's power over obscenity, and that excluding expert testimony on contemporary community standards violated due process. Justice Douglas concurred, adhering to his Roth dissent that obscenity may not be punished absent a showing of close connection to illegal action, and seeing some value in a scienter rule. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part: he would not reach the scienter question on the thin record and considered the Court's balancing too rough, but would reverse because the trial court refused all evidence on community standards.","disposition":"The judgment of the California court was reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep361/usrep361147/usrep361147.pdf"},"1940-1955/339us382":{"facts":"Section 9(h) of the National Labor Relations Act, added by the Labor Management Relations Act of 1947, denied the Board's processes to any union unless each officer filed an affidavit that he was not a member of or affiliated with the Communist Party and did not believe in or support any organization that believed in overthrowing the government by force or illegal or unconstitutional methods. Congress had heard evidence that Communists and others had used union leadership to call political strikes obstructing commerce, such as a 1941 strike at an Allis-Chalmers plant producing defense materials. In No. 10 a union whose officers had not filed sued to restrain an election from which its name would be excluded; in No. 13, steelworkers' unions faced the Board postponing an order in their favor until they complied.","procedure":"In No. 10 a three-judge district court dismissed the union's complaint, with one judge dissenting. In No. 13 the Seventh Circuit upheld the Board's postponement of its order pending compliance, with one judge dissenting on section 9(h). The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction in No. 10 and granted certiorari in No. 13 on the constitutional question.","question":"Does section 9(h) of the National Labor Relations Act, which conditions a union's access to the Board on non-Communist affidavits from its officers, violate the First Amendment, the Fifth Amendment, the ban on bills of attainder and ex post facto laws, or the bar on religious test oaths?","holding":"No. Congress may under its commerce power seek to prevent political strikes, and section 9(h) bears a reasonable relation to that evil. The statute reaches conduct that Congress concluded Communists and advocates of forced overthrow were likely to engage in, and it does not unduly infringe freedoms protected by the First Amendment or violate the other constitutional provisions raised.","reasoning":"The section does more than withdraw a privilege, since noncomplying unions face restrictions they would not otherwise bear, yet it does not forbid anyone from holding union office, so it falls between a licensing law and a withheld benefit. Congress could rationally find that Communists, unlike members of other parties, and persons who believe in forcible overthrow, unlike persons of other beliefs, pose a continuing danger of political strikes when in union leadership, as with statutes barring securities underwriters from bank directorships. The Court declined to apply clear and present danger as a mechanical test, and instead weighed the burden on political rights against Congress's finding that political strikes are substantial evils of conduct that harm interstate commerce. Because the provision touches relatively few persons who hold great power over the economy and because injury would be done before any sanction could apply, the legislative judgment was permissible. The belief covered is belief in overthrowing the government by force or illegal methods, and the statute's only effect on such a person is that he may have to give up union office. The statute is not unconstitutionally vague, is not a bill of attainder or ex post facto law since officers can resume office by renouncing the proscribed ties, and imposes no religious test oath.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter concurred in all of the Court's opinion except Part VII, arguing that the oath, in its coverage of beliefs and uncertain affiliations, probes men's thoughts and that he would, if he could, construe the statute to reach only actual membership in or control by the Communist Party. Justice Jackson concurred in part and dissented in part, distinguishing the Communist Party from other parties as a justification for the statute while limiting his concurrence to those distinctions, and he would not sustain the portion directed at beliefs. Justice Black dissented, arguing that the First Amendment protects freedom of belief against a government test of the inward workings of the mind.","disposition":"Affirmed in both cases, in an opinion by Chief Justice Vinson; Justices Douglas, Clark and Minton took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep339/usrep339382/usrep339382.pdf"},"1957/7":{"facts":"Black members of a railway clerks' union, which was the designated bargaining agent under the Railway Labor Act, alleged that the Railroad purported to abolish 45 jobs held by Black employees but in fact filled them with white workers, except for a few cases in which Black employees were rehired with loss of seniority. They alleged that the union, acting according to plan, did nothing to protect them despite repeated pleas and refused them the protection it gave white employees. They sought declaratory relief, an injunction and damages.","procedure":"The employees sued as a class in federal district court in Texas against the union, its local and certain officers. The district court dismissed on the ground that the National Railroad Adjustment Board had exclusive jurisdiction, and the Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit affirmed.","question":"Did the Railway Labor Act give the Adjustment Board exclusive jurisdiction over this suit, was the Railroad an indispensable party, and did the complaint state a claim on which relief could be granted?","holding":"The Adjustment Board's jurisdiction covers only disputes between employees and carriers, so it did not bar this suit against the bargaining agent, and the Railroad was not an indispensable party. The complaint stated a claim for breach of the union's duty of fair representation, and the Federal Rules require only a short and plain statement giving fair notice, not detailed facts.","reasoning":"Section 3 First (i) of the Act applies by its terms only to disputes between employees and carriers, whereas this was a suit by employees against their bargaining agent to enforce a statutory right not to be discriminated against, and the Board has no power to remedy that. The Railroad's rights were not affected because no relief was sought from it. Under Steele and later cases, a bargaining agent must represent all unit members fairly, and that duty continues after a contract is made because bargaining includes day-to-day adjustments and protecting contract rights. The ability of employees to file their own grievances or sue the employer does not excuse discrimination by the union. A complaint should not be dismissed unless it appears beyond doubt that the plaintiff can prove no set of facts entitling him to relief, and the Rules reject pleading as a game of skill in favor of notice pleading supported by discovery and pretrial procedures.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the district court for further proceedings. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state a vote or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep355/usrep355041/usrep355041.pdf"},"1956/175":{"facts":"Paul Sweezy, a self-described classical Marxist and socialist, was summoned twice in 1954 by the New Hampshire Attorney General, whom the legislature had authorized to investigate violations of the state's Subversive Activities Act and to determine whether subversive persons were present in the state. Sweezy answered most questions, denying Communist Party membership and any advocacy of violent overthrow. He refused to answer questions about the contents of a lecture he gave to a University of New Hampshire class and about the Progressive Party and its members, and one question on whether he believed in Communism. He did not invoke the privilege against self-incrimination but said the questions were not pertinent and violated the First Amendment.","procedure":"The Attorney General petitioned a state superior court, which ruled the lecture and Progressive Party questions pertinent, and when Sweezy persisted in refusing to answer, held him in contempt and committed him to jail until he complied. The New Hampshire Supreme Court affirmed. The appeal to the U.S. Supreme Court was dismissed for failure to show jurisdiction by appeal, but the papers were treated as a petition for certiorari, which was granted.","question":"Did compelling Sweezy, under threat of contempt, to answer the Attorney General's questions about his lecture and the Progressive Party deny him due process under the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. On this record the Court found no basis for the state's power to compel the disclosures, and the contempt judgment violated the Due Process Clause. There was no majority opinion; four Justices rested on the lack of a shown legislative authorization for the questions, and two concurred on First Amendment grounds.","reasoning":"The plurality opinion of the Chief Justice said legislative investigations can encroach on liberty, especially in academic freedom and political association, and that the compelled disclosure here was an invasion of those rights. It declined to decide whether any state interest could justify that, because the state court's reasoning had a missing link between the questions and the state's interest in self-protection. The legislature had given the Attorney General a sweeping mandate keyed to a broad definition of subversive persons that reached remote conduct without any guilty knowledge, so he alone selected subjects, witnesses and questions. Nothing assured that the legislature wanted the information sought from Sweezy, and that absence had to be treated as an absence of authority, so contempt in the face of constitutional interests denied due process. The plurality stressed that its conclusion did not rest on separation of powers. Justice Frankfurter, concurring in the result, treated the case as if the full legislature had asked and weighed the competing interests directly.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter, joined by Justice Harlan, concurred in the result, rejecting the plurality's focus on the delegation within the state government and reasoning that the compelled disclosure of a university lecture's contents and of political associations in a lawful party offended due process, since the state's countervailing interest was too slight and the intrusion into academic and political freedom required a compelling justification. Justice Clark, joined by Justice Burton, dissented, arguing that the state court's construction showed the legislature had directed the inquiry, the plurality's theory was novel and unraised by the parties, Wieman v. Updegraff was misread, and the state's interest in investigating subversion had not been shown to be outweighed.","disposition":"Reversed. Chief Justice Warren announced the judgment and delivered an opinion joined by Justices Black, Douglas and Brennan; Justices Frankfurter and Harlan concurred in the result, Justices Clark and Burton dissented, and Justice Whittaker took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep354/usrep354234/usrep354234.pdf"},"1956/211":{"facts":"The Textile Workers Union and Lincoln Mills of Alabama made a one-year collective bargaining agreement in 1953 that renewed from year to year. It barred strikes and work stoppages and set up a grievance procedure whose last step, available to either side, was arbitration. Several grievances about work loads and work assignments were processed and finally denied by the employer, and when the union asked for arbitration the employer refused. Some grievances also sought back pay, and after the Court of Appeals ruled the employer ceased operations and contracted to sell its mill.","procedure":"The union sued in federal district court under section 301 of the Labor Management Relations Act of 1947 to compel arbitration. The District Court held it had jurisdiction and ordered the employer to arbitrate. A divided Court of Appeals reversed, holding that the District Court had jurisdiction but lacked authority under federal or state law to grant that relief. The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of the importance of the problem and the conflicting views in the courts.","question":"Is section 301(a) of the Labor Management Relations Act merely a grant of jurisdiction, or does it authorize federal courts to fashion federal substantive law, including specific enforcement of a collective bargaining agreement's promise to arbitrate grievances?","holding":"Section 301(a) is more than jurisdictional. It authorizes federal courts to fashion a body of federal law from the policy of the national labor laws for enforcing collective bargaining agreements, and that law includes specific performance of promises to arbitrate grievances. The Norris-LaGuardia Act does not bar such relief, and the section is constitutional as construed.","reasoning":"Section 301(b) supplies the procedural ability of a union to sue and be sued as an entity, and section 301(a) must do something more. The legislative history is cloudy, but it shows that Congress dropped a proposal to make breach of such agreements an unfair labor practice in favor of enforcement through the usual processes of law, and that it wanted agreements enforceable in court by either party. The agreement to arbitrate grievances was treated as the counterpart of an agreement not to strike, so Congress adopted a policy of placing sanctions behind arbitration agreements, impliedly rejecting the common-law rule against enforcing executory agreements to arbitrate. Reading the section as only jurisdictional would undercut the Act. The substantive law is federal and is to be worked out by the courts from labor policy, and state law may be used only if compatible with that policy, in which case it is absorbed as federal law. There is no constitutional difficulty because a case under section 301(a) arises under federal law within Article III and Congress has commerce power over these disputes. Although a literal reading of the Norris-LaGuardia Act might reach this relief, failure to arbitrate was not among the abuses it targeted, the Act itself favors arbitration, and earlier decisions did not apply it to bar enforcement of federal labor mandates. The case is moot as to restoring work loads and job assignments but not as to the claims for money.","separate":"Justice Burton, joined by Justice Harlan, concurred in the result. He thought the court could order arbitration under section 301 and its inherent equitable powers, but did not agree that the substantive law in a section 301 suit is federal; he would sustain the section as a grant of protective jurisdiction because some federal rights may be involved. Justice Frankfurter dissented, arguing that section 301 is purely procedural and its history does not support a mandate to create federal substantive labor law; that even if it did, the 1925 Arbitration Act's exclusion of employment contracts and the common-law rule against enforcing arbitration agreements preclude specific enforcement; and that if section 301 supplies the sole basis for jurisdiction over state-law contract claims, it exceeds Article III and is unconstitutional. He attached an appendix reviewing the legislative history.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Black took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep353/usrep353448/usrep353448.pdf"},"1956/92":{"facts":"Rudolph Schware applied in 1953 to take the New Mexico bar examination after finishing law school. The Board of Bar Examiners refused to let him sit for it, saying that because of his use of aliases, his former connection with subversive organizations, and his record of arrests, he had not satisfied the Board as to his moral character. Schware had used aliases from 1933 to 1937 mainly to avoid anti-Jewish discrimination in hiring and organizing, had been arrested several times before 1940 without trial or conviction, and had belonged to the Communist Party from 1932 to 1940. He also served honorably in the Army, supported his family while in law school, and was vouched for by many witnesses, and no one testified that he lacked good character. The State conceded he was qualified in all other respects.","procedure":"The Board held a formal hearing and reaffirmed its decision. The New Mexico Supreme Court upheld the denial, with one justice dissenting, relying on the Communist Party membership together with the aliases, arrests and his present attitude toward them. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did New Mexico deny Schware due process under the Fourteenth Amendment by refusing to let him take the bar examination for lack of good moral character on this record?","holding":"Yes. On this record the State deprived Schware of due process by denying him the opportunity to qualify for the practice of law. His aliases, arrests and past Communist Party membership, alone or combined, did not rationally support a finding that he lacked good moral character.","reasoning":"A State may not exclude a person from a profession in a way that violates due process or equal protection. It may require good moral character, but any qualification must have a rational connection with fitness to practice law, and officials cannot exclude an applicant without a basis for their finding. Schware's showing of good character was strong and uncontradicted. His aliases were adopted to meet employment discrimination, not to defraud, and were too remote to show present bad character. An arrest has very little probative value of misconduct, and these arrests were more than 20 years old, none led to conviction, and the 1934 syndicalism arrests came during mass strike arrests. The Neutrality Act charge was dropped and did not indicate moral turpitude. Communist Party membership from 1932 to 1940 did not justify an inference of present bad character, because the Party was then a lawful political party and nothing showed he took part in illegal acts or shared illegal aims, and \"Indiscriminate classification of innocent with knowing activity must fall as an assertion of arbitrary power.\" Combining the three items did not raise substantial doubts about his present character. The Court did not decide his procedural claim about secret evidence or whether the practice of law is a right or privilege.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter, joined by Justices Clark and Harlan, concurred in the judgment. He stressed the bar's historic role and the State's wide latitude over moral character, and said the Court should not act as overseer of individual admission decisions. He found a due process violation because the New Mexico court's treatment of Schware's early Communist affiliation as itself making him of questionable character was so dogmatic an inference as to be wholly unwarranted and arbitrary.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Whittaker took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep353/usrep353232/usrep353232.pdf"},"1940-1955/350us11":{"facts":"Robert Toth served in the Air Force in Korea and was honorably discharged. He returned home to Pittsburgh and worked in a steel plant. Five months later military authorities arrested him on charges of murder and conspiracy to commit murder allegedly committed while he was an airman in Korea. When arrested he had no relationship of any kind with the military, yet he was taken to Korea for trial by court-martial under Article 3(a) of the Uniform Code of Military Justice, a 1950 Act of Congress.","procedure":"His sister brought a habeas corpus action; the District Court ordered his discharge on a non-constitutional ground, that he should not have been carried to Korea without a hearing. The Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit reversed and sustained the Act, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari on the constitutional question.","question":"May Congress constitutionally subject a civilian ex-serviceman, who has severed all relationship with the military, to trial by court-martial for an offense allegedly committed while he was in the armed forces?","holding":"No. Article 3(a) is not a valid exercise of Congress's power to make rules for the government and regulation of the land and naval forces, even read with the Necessary and Proper Clause. Congress cannot subject civilians like Toth to trial by court-martial, and they are entitled to the safeguards of the regular Article III courts.","reasoning":"The Court first said the Act could not rest on the powers to raise armies, declare war, or punish offenses against the law of nations, nor on the Commander-in-Chief power or martial law. Its prior cases had allowed court-martial of persons actually in service, and of a dishonorably discharged soldier still serving a military sentence, but never of civilian ex-soldiers who had severed all ties. The Court read the power to regulate \"the land and naval Forces\" naturally as limited to actual members of the forces, and found a compelling reason for that reading: any expansion of court-martial jurisdiction \"necessarily encroaches on the jurisdiction of federal courts\" set up under Article III, where defendants have more safeguards, including independent judges, grand jury indictment and jury trial. Military tribunals are not equal to those courts as adjudicators of guilt, since their members are appointed and removable by commanders and are not insulated like judges, and the Act would reach millions of veterans for a great range of offenses. Congress could instead have provided for trial of discharged soldiers in federal district courts, so they need not go untried; military discipline would not be harmed by civilian trial; and the Necessary and Proper Clause should not be used to circumvent Bill of Rights safeguards, since military jurisdiction is to be limited to the narrowest scope essential to discipline.","separate":"Justice Reed, joined by Justices Burton and Minton, dissented. He argued that Congress's power to regulate the armed forces, with the Necessary and Proper Clause, reaches crimes committed while in service even if charged after discharge; that the Fifth Amendment exception for \"cases arising in the land or naval forces\" covers a case that arises from a military offense; that history and statutes supported post-discharge court-martial; that the choice between military and civilian trial belonged to Congress; and that the decision turned loose an accused murderer. Justice Minton, joined by Justice Burton, added that Toth was not a full-fledged civilian because Congress had expressly retained court-martial jurisdiction over him by statute, as he held only a conditional discharge.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text shows dissents by Justices Reed, Burton, and Minton, and does not state the vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep350/usrep350011/usrep350011.pdf"},"1956/28":{"facts":"Petitioner, a laborer in a railroad section gang, was assigned for the first time to burn weeds along the respondent's double-track line near Garner, Arkansas, using a crude hand torch, though he testified burning was customarily done with a flame thrower from a car on the tracks. His foreman had instructed the crew to stop work when a train passed and stand off the tracks to watch for hotboxes. When a train approached he ran to a narrow path near a culvert; the passing train fanned the flames of the burning weeds so that he was enveloped in smoke and fire, and as he retreated he slipped and fell from the culvert, which was covered with loose, sloping gravel, and was seriously injured.","procedure":"A jury in the Circuit Court of St. Louis awarded petitioner damages under the Federal Employers' Liability Act. The Missouri Supreme Court reversed on the ground that his evidence did not support a finding of liability, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to decide whether that decision invaded the jury's function.","question":"Was the evidence sufficient to submit to the jury, and sustain its verdict, that the railroad's negligence played a part in petitioner's injury under the Federal Employers' Liability Act?","holding":"Yes. Under the Act the test of a jury case is whether the proofs justify with reason the conclusion that employer negligence played any part, even the slightest, in producing the injury, and it does not matter that the jury could also attribute the result to other causes. The evidence was sufficient, and the Missouri court wrongly took the decision from the jury.","reasoning":"The Court found ample evidence of negligence: the customary use of flame throwers from cars, rather than a man on foot with a hand torch, together with the uncontradicted testimony that petitioner stood on the narrow path in obedience to orders to watch for hotboxes, supported a finding that the railroad should have known of conditions likely to cause such an injury. Common experience teaches that a passing train fans flames and that a person suddenly enveloped in fire will retreat without heeding other dangers, so it was irrelevant whether the immediate cause of the fall was the gravel or something else. The Missouri court had treated petitioner's own inattention as the sole cause, but his testimony left at best an uncertainty of fact for the jury, and the jury had been instructed to find for the railroad if his negligence was the sole cause. The Missouri court also required that the injury would not have occurred but for the employer's negligence, which is a proximate-cause test inconsistent with the statute's \"in whole or in part\" language. The Court explained that the Act stripped employers of common-law defenses and that Congress meant the jury to decide whenever fair-minded people could find employer fault played any part. It added that the Court must prevent erosion of the Act by \"narrow and niggardly construction\" and give FELA litigants the same measure of review as in other cases.","separate":"The text provided shows that Justice Burton concurred in the result and Justice Reed would have affirmed the Missouri Supreme Court. It also refers to a dissenting opinion by Justice Frankfurter and an opinion by Justice Harlan dissenting in this case, but those opinions appear later in the U.S. Reports and are not included in the text provided, so their reasoning cannot be briefed here.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; the text does not state the vote count, noting Burton concurring in the result, Reed for affirmance, and dissents by Justices Frankfurter and Harlan.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep352/usrep352500/usrep352500.pdf"},"1958/583":{"facts":"Henry Napue was tried for murder in an Illinois state court. The principal state witness was George Hamer, an accomplice then serving a 199-year sentence for the same killing, whose testimony made up most of the State's evidence. On cross-examination and again on redirect by the Assistant State's Attorney, Hamer denied that anyone had promised him anything for testifying. In fact the Assistant State's Attorney had promised to recommend a reduction of Hamer's sentence, and he did nothing to correct the false answer. The jury did hear Hamer say that a public defender had said he would try to help him.","procedure":"The trial court denied Napue post-conviction relief on the prosecutor's testimony. The Illinois Supreme Court affirmed on different grounds over two dissents: it found the promise had been made and the prosecutor knew Hamer lied, but held there was no constitutional defect because the jury already knew of the public defender's offer. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a prosecutor's failure to correct testimony from a key state witness that he knows to be false, where the falsehood concerns whether the witness was promised consideration, deny the defendant due process under the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. A conviction obtained through false evidence known to be false by state representatives violates the Fourteenth Amendment, and the same is true when the State allows such evidence to go uncorrected. The rule applies even when the falsehood goes only to a witness's credibility.","reasoning":"The Court began with the established rule that a State may not knowingly use false testimony, or let it stand uncorrected, to obtain a conviction. It held that this principle does not cease to apply merely because the false testimony bears only on the witness's credibility, since the jury's view of a witness's reliability and possible interest may decide guilt or innocence. It rejected the Illinois court's view that the jury's knowledge of the public defender's offer cured the problem: Hamer had testified that no one but an unidentified public defender had offered help, and the jury might have viewed his testimony differently had it known the prosecutor himself had made a promise. Finally, the Court said it is not bound by a state court's factual conclusion on a federal constitutional claim and must examine the record itself. Its own review showed that the false testimony may have affected the outcome.","disposition":"Reversed. The opinion of the Court was delivered by Chief Justice Warren; the text shown does not state a vote count or any separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep360/usrep360264/usrep360264.pdf"},"1955/58":{"facts":"Ullmann was subpoenaed before a federal grand jury in the Southern District of New York investigating espionage and conspiracy to commit espionage. Invoking the privilege against self-incrimination, he refused to answer questions about his knowledge of such activities, about his and others' participation in them, and about Communist Party membership. The United States Attorney, with the Attorney General's approval, applied under the Immunity Act of 1954 for an order compelling him to testify. The Act bars prosecution, penalty or forfeiture for matters about which a witness is compelled to testify and bars use of the compelled testimony.","procedure":"The District Court upheld the statute's constitutionality and ordered Ullmann to testify; the Court of Appeals dismissed his appeal from that order. When he again refused, he was convicted of contempt and sentenced to six months, and the Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Immunity Act of 1954 violate the Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination, or exceed Congress's power or the judicial role, by compelling testimony in exchange for immunity from prosecution?","holding":"The Act is constitutional and the contempt conviction stands. Immunity from prosecution, penalties and forfeitures is broad enough to displace the privilege, so compelled testimony under the Act does not violate the Fifth Amendment, and Brown v. Walker is reaffirmed.","reasoning":"The Court began by insisting that the privilege be read generously rather than in a hostile spirit. It then followed Brown v. Walker, which upheld an earlier immunity statute, and held that immunity need only remove the sanctions that generate the fear justifying the privilege; collateral consequences such as loss of employment, passport difficulties or public opprobrium are not criminal sanctions the privilege protects against. Reading subsection (c) to leave the district judge no discretion to deny the application if the statutory requirements are met, the Court found that the judge performs only a judicial function and avoided a separation-of-powers question. It read the Act to give immunity against state prosecution as well and held that Congress could do so under its national defense and Necessary and Proper Clause powers. It added that \"Immunity displaces the danger.\" The Court declined to overrule Brown v. Walker, which had been treated as settled for sixty years, and noted that petitioner's First Amendment argument was not properly raised.","separate":"Justice Reed concurred in the opinion and judgment except as to the statement that no constitutional guarantee enjoys preference. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Black, dissented. He would reverse under Boyd v. United States because the Act does not protect against disabilities and forfeitures imposed by federal law on Communists, or else overrule Brown v. Walker, arguing that the Fifth Amendment protects against prosecution, conscience, dignity and infamy as well as conviction, so that Congress \"has granted far less than it has taken away.\"","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Frankfurter delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shows Justice Reed concurring in part and Justices Douglas and Black dissenting, and does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep350/usrep350422/usrep350422.pdf"},"1957/59":{"facts":"Congress granted the Northern Pacific's predecessor about forty million acres in the Northwest to help build its railroad. The railroad sold most of that land and leased much of the rest. Many sales contracts and most leases contained \"preferential routing\" clauses requiring the buyer or lessee to ship goods produced on the land over the railroad's lines, provided its rates (and sometimes service) were equal to those of competing carriers. Much of the produce was shipped across state lines, and other carriers existed for many of the shipments.","procedure":"In 1949 the United States sued under Section 4 of the Sherman Act for a declaration that the clauses were unreasonable restraints of trade under Section 1. The District Court granted the Government summary judgment and enjoined enforcement of existing clauses and use of future ones. The railroad appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Are the railroad's preferential routing clauses, which tie the sale or lease of land to shipping on its lines, unlawful per se under Section 1 of the Sherman Act, and was summary judgment proper on this record?","holding":"Yes. A tying arrangement is unreasonable in itself whenever the seller has sufficient economic power over the tying product to restrain free competition appreciably in the tied product and a not insubstantial amount of interstate commerce is affected. Those prerequisites were established here, so the judgment is affirmed.","reasoning":"The Court explained that some practices, including tying arrangements, are \"conclusively presumed to be unreasonable and therefore illegal\" without a detailed inquiry into their effects. Tying denies competitors access to the tied market because of the seller's leverage in another market, and forces buyers to give up a free choice. The undisputed facts showed the railroad's substantial economic power through its extensive, strategically located land holdings, and the large number of clauses was itself strong evidence of that power. The clauses gave buyers no benefit, and their purpose was to fence out competitors. The Court relied on International Salt, rejected the argument that it turned on the tying product being patented, and read Times-Picayune as requiring only sufficient economic power rather than monopoly. It also held that exceptions letting buyers use rival carriers with lower rates or better service do not avoid the clauses' stifling effect.","separate":"Justice Harlan, joined by Justices Frankfurter and Whittaker, dissented. He argued that the District Court made no proper finding of dominance in the relevant land market, that Times-Picayune requires proof of market dominance and substantial restraint, and that the case should be remanded for trial. He also objected that the majority's treatment of International Salt and of summary judgment would produce confusion.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court. Justice Clark took no part. Justice Harlan dissented, joined by Justices Frankfurter and Whittaker; the text does not state a count beyond that.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep356/usrep356001/usrep356001.pdf"},"1957/668":{"facts":"Gore was indicted on six counts for narcotics offenses arising from two sales, one on February 26, 1955 (heroin and cocaine) and one on February 28, 1955 (heroin). For each sale he was charged under three separate provisions: selling not in pursuance of a written order (Int. Rev. Code § 4705(a)), selling not from the original stamped package (§ 4704(a)), and facilitating concealment and sale of drugs knowing they were unlawfully imported (§ 2(c) of the Narcotic Drugs Import and Export Act). A jury convicted him.","procedure":"The trial court imposed one-to-five-year terms on each count, the first three to run consecutively and the other three concurrently with them, for a total of three to fifteen years. Gore moved under 28 U.S.C. § 2255 to vacate the sentences, arguing that only one sentence per sale could be imposed. The district court denied the motion and the Court of Appeals affirmed, two judges expressing doubt but feeling bound by Blockburger; the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a defendant who made a single sale of narcotics be convicted and given cumulative sentences under three separate statutory provisions, each of which the sale violated, or must the sale be treated as one offense; and does Blockburger v. United States survive later decisions and the Double Jeopardy Clause?","holding":"The cumulative sentences were lawful. The Court adhered to Blockburger: where Congress has created distinct offenses in separate provisions enacted at different times, a single transaction that violates each may be punished separately, and this does not offend the Double Jeopardy Clause.","reasoning":"Justice Frankfurter reasoned that the three provisions had different origins in time and design, forming a network of laws that Congress progressively tightened, so it was unlikely Congress meant a single sale to count as one offense. Bell v. United States, which applied lenity to a single Mann Act provision, was different, because here Congress showed \"an attitude not of lenity but of severity\" toward narcotics violations. Other cases relied on by the petitioner were distinguished. On double jeopardy, a long line of this Court's decisions would have to be overruled if separate punishment for separate offenses were forbidden, and Congress could clearly have enacted a single statute with graded penalties. Apportioning punishment is a matter of legislative policy, and the Court has no power to revise sentences.","separate":"Chief Justice Warren dissented, arguing that the question is what the legislature intended, that formulaic presumptions are inappropriate, and that the origins, amendments, and penalty scale of the three statutes show they give the prosecutor three avenues to prosecute one sale rather than authorizing three cumulative punishments. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Black, dissented, would overrule Blockburger on the basis of his views on double jeopardy, and would hold that the prosecutor could choose one of three prosecutions for a single sale. Justice Brennan dissented, arguing that even under Blockburger's test the decision was inconsistent, because the statutory presumptions meant proof of the single fact of possession of unstamped narcotics could convict under both § 4704(a) and § 174.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Frankfurter delivered the opinion of the Court; Chief Justice Warren, Justice Douglas (joined by Justice Black), and Justice Brennan dissented. The opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep357/usrep357386/usrep357386.pdf"},"1958/175":{"facts":"Four appellants (Raley, Stern, Brown, and Mrs. Morgan) were summoned before Ohio's Un-American Activities Commission, a legislative body, and questioned about Communist and subversive activities. Each refused to answer many questions by claiming the privilege against self-incrimination. The Commission's chairman told or signaled to them that the privilege was available, and the Commission generally did not press questions it thought incriminating. Ohio had an immunity statute that, as later construed by the state supreme court, removed the privilege, but no one told the appellants of it.","procedure":"Each appellant was convicted of refusing to answer. The Ohio Court of Appeals and trial courts relied on various theories, but the Ohio Supreme Court affirmed on the sole ground that the immunity statute made the privilege unavailable, so the appellants were presumed to know it and no direction to answer was needed. The cases had been before the U.S. Supreme Court earlier and were remanded in light of Sweezy and Watkins; the Ohio court reaffirmed, and the appeals came back. The Court postponed the jurisdictional question to the merits.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment permit a State to convict witnesses for refusing to answer legislative commission questions on grounds of self-incrimination when the commission had led them to believe the privilege was available to them?","holding":"No. Convicting witnesses for relying on a privilege that the State's own commission had assured them was available violates due process, so the convictions of Raley, Brown, and Morgan were reversed. The Court was equally divided on Stern's conviction, so it was affirmed. The appeals were dismissed for want of jurisdiction under § 1257(2), but the papers were treated as petitions for certiorari and certiorari was granted.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the appellants had not shown that they attacked the validity of a state statute in the state courts, so appeal did not lie, but federal rights were claimed below and the Ohio Supreme Court passed on the due process claim, which sufficed for certiorari. On the merits, the Chairman told three appellants the privilege existed and behaved the same way toward the fourth, and other members and counsel acted as though the immunity statute did not exist. Justice Brennan wrote that sustaining the convictions would \"sanction the most indefensible sort of entrapment by the State\", and that there had been active misleading rather than mere vagueness. The Court refused to excuse the defect by speculating that the appellants would have been defiant anyway, or by dividing the inquiry into segments before and after the Chairman's explicit assurances. As to Stern, who was directed to answer one question about his residence, four Justices thought the direction was given because the Commission believed the privilege did not reach that question, not because of the immunity statute, and the Ohio Supreme Court had not decided otherwise.","separate":"Justice Clark, joined by Justices Frankfurter, Harlan, and Whittaker, concurred in the reversals for Brown, Raley, and Morgan but would have affirmed Stern's conviction, because Stern was categorically ordered to answer the question about his residence and so was not entrapped. Justice Stewart took no part.","disposition":"Appeals dismissed; on certiorari, judgments reversed as to Raley, Brown, and Morgan, and affirmed as to Stern by an equally divided Court. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court (four Justices joined him on the Stern question; the text does not name them); Justice Stewart took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep360/usrep360423/usrep360423.pdf"},"1958/180":{"facts":"Greene, an aeronautical engineer, was vice president and general manager of a private corporation (ERCO) that did classified work for the armed services; his job required access to classified information. In 1951 and 1952 his clearance was revoked and then restored on appeal. In April 1953 the Secretary of the Navy again directed that he be barred from classified work without a hearing, and ERCO discharged him. Later hearings before a regional board relied on confidential reports from informants he could not confront, and focused on his associations during 1942-1947 with his former wife and her Communist friends, visits to embassies, and similar matters. He was unable to find work in aeronautics.","procedure":"Greene sued in the District of Columbia for a declaration that the revocation was unlawful and an order restraining the Secretaries from acting on it. The District Court granted summary judgment for the Government, and the Court of Appeals affirmed, reasoning that the matter was not justiciable and that he had not been denied due process. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the President or Congress authorize the Department of Defense to run an industrial security clearance program under which a person may lose his job and be barred from his chosen profession on the basis of confidential informants' statements, without the safeguards of confrontation and cross-examination?","holding":"No. In the absence of explicit authorization from either the President or Congress, the respondents were not empowered to deprive Greene of his job in a proceeding in which he was not afforded confrontation and cross-examination. The Court did not decide the constitutional questions.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Warren decided the case on the narrower ground of authorization rather than the Fifth Amendment due process questions. He noted that where government action seriously injures a person and depends on fact findings, evidence must be disclosed so that it can be tested, a principle embodied in confrontation and cross-examination. The Defense Department's programs came from directives of the Secretary of Defense and the service Secretaries, not from statute or a presidential order. Executive Orders 10290 and 10501 only concerned safeguarding and disseminating classified information, and the National Security Act, the Armed Services Procurement Act, and the criminal statutes on disclosure did not authorize such a program. Continued appropriations did not show ratification, since Congress was never shown the procedures. Acquiescence cannot supply authority in an area of questionable constitutionality; such decisions must be made explicitly by the President or Congress. The Court did hold that the Department was authorized to run a program that affords confrontation and cross-examination.","separate":"Justices Frankfurter, Harlan, and Whittaker concurred in the judgment on the ground that it had not been shown that Congress or the President authorized the procedures, expressing no view on their validity. Justice Harlan also concurred specially, saying that he could not join the Court's opinion because it unnecessarily dealt with the very constitutional issue it disclaimed deciding. Justice Clark dissented, arguing that no one has a constitutional right to the Government's secrets, that administrative proceedings have long been approved without confrontation, and that Presidents and Congress had sufficiently authorized and been informed of the program for years.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court. Chief Justice Warren delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Frankfurter, Harlan, and Whittaker concurred in the judgment; Justice Clark dissented. The opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep360/usrep360474/usrep360474.pdf"},"1955/19":{"facts":"Mastro Plastics and French-American Reeds were affiliated New York manufacturers whose employees were represented by the Carpenters under a one-year contract containing a clause that the union would \"refrain from engaging in any strike or work stoppage\" during the term. In 1950 a rival union sought to organize the plant, and the employers pressed employees to join another union and, on November 10, discharged Ciccone for supporting the Carpenters. That discharge precipitated a strike that the Board found was caused by the employers' unfair labor practices and not aimed at changing the contract. The Carpenters had earlier given written notice asking to modify the contract, which started the 60-day period of section 8(d) of the National Labor Relations Act. The strike lasted until March 1951, and the employers refused to reinstate the strikers.","procedure":"The Board found unfair labor practices under § 8(a)(1), (2) and (3) and ordered reinstatement of Ciccone and 76 other strikers with back pay, and the Second Circuit enforced the order, with one judge dissenting in part. The employers defended on two grounds: that the contract waived the right to strike and that the strikers lost employee status under § 8(d) by striking in the 60-day period. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the no-strike clause waive employees' right to strike solely against employers' unfair labor practices, and did § 8(d) strip strikers of employee status for striking during the 60-day waiting period in a strike solely protesting unfair labor practices?","holding":"No to both. The no-strike clause did not waive the right to strike solely against unfair labor practices, and the loss-of-status provision of § 8(d) does not apply to a strike aimed solely at protesting the employers' unfair labor practices rather than at terminating or modifying the contract.","reasoning":"The Court assumed the right could have been waived by explicit contract language or removed by explicit statute, and asked whether either had occurred. It read the contract as a whole and concluded that \"the contract, taken as a whole, deals solely with the economic relationship between the employers and their employees\", so the clause covers economic strikes and cannot be extended without a more compelling expression to a waiver that would allow employers to oust the union by coercion without any strike response. Section 13 of the Act does not change this and, if anything, underscores the recognized propriety of unfair-practice strikes. On § 8(d), the Court read the loss-of-status clause with paragraph (4) and the words \"termination or modification\", so it targets strikes over the subjects of negotiation, and the Board found this strike had no such purpose. The employers' reading would create incongruous results by penalizing employees for giving notice and by rewarding the wrongdoer, and a limit on the right to strike against violations of sections 7 and 8(a) must be more explicit. The legislative history is not conclusive but is consistent with this reading.","separate":"Justice Frankfurter dissented, joined by Justices Minton and Harlan. He agreed that the no-strike clause did not cover this strike but argued that § 8(d)'s loss-of-status provision should be given its ordinary meaning. In his view the provision has practical effect only for unfair-labor-practice strikers, since economic strikers in the period already had no right to reinstatement, and Congress may have chosen to deter all strikes in the negotiating period and avoid litigation over what caused them.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Burton delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shows three Justices dissenting and does not state the full vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep350/usrep350270/usrep350270.pdf"},"1957/99":{"facts":"Payne, a mentally dull 19-year-old with a fifth-grade education, was suspected of the fatal beating of a lumber dealer in Pine Bluff, Arkansas. He was arrested without a warrant, never taken before a magistrate, and held incommunicado for about three days without counsel, advisor or friend; family members were turned away and his request for a telephone call was refused. He was fed very little. On the third day the chief of police told him that \"there would be 30 or 40 people there in a few minutes that wanted to get him\" and that he would probably keep them away if Payne confessed. Payne then confessed and signed a transcribed statement.","procedure":"The trial court denied a motion to suppress, admitted the confession over objection, and a jury convicted Payne of first degree murder and sentenced him to death. The Arkansas Supreme Court affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the admission at a state criminal trial of a confession shown to have been coerced, here by a threat of mob violence amid prolonged incommunicado detention, deny the defendant due process of law under the Fourteenth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. Considering the totality of the conduct shown by the undisputed evidence, the confession was coerced, and its admission over objection denied due process. Admission of a coerced confession vitiates the conviction even if other evidence might have been enough to support it.","reasoning":"The Court said that use of a coerced confession, whether the coercion is physical or mental, is forbidden by the Fourteenth Amendment, and that on a claim of coercion it must make its own examination of the record rather than defer to state findings. It reviewed the undisputed facts: warrantless arrest, no hearing before a magistrate, no advice of rights, three days incommunicado, little food, and the chief's statement about the crowd. It concluded from this course of conduct, and especially the culminating threat of mob violence, that the confession did not constitute an \"expression of free choice.\" It rejected the State's argument that other evidence sufficed, because with a general verdict no one can say what weight the jury gave the confession. It distinguished Stein v. New York as a case in which the confession was not found coerced, and did not reach the jury-selection claim.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred, joining the reversal because the police chief's own testimony required accepting that the confession was induced by fear of mob violence. Justice Burton would have accepted the state court and jury's conclusion that the confession was voluntary and would have affirmed. Justice Clark dissented, believing the confession voluntary and, in any event, that other evidence of guilt sufficed under Stein v. New York because Arkansas procedure matched that approved in Stein.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Whittaker delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Harlan (concurring), Burton and Clark (dissenting) wrote separately, and the opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep356/usrep356560/usrep356560.pdf"},"1957/331":{"facts":"Federal alcohol agents learned that Jones's farmhouse in Georgia housed an illicit still. Over several days they found spent mash, traced a hose toward the house, smelled hot mash, and heard a blower burner and voices inside. An officer obtained a daytime search warrant but chose to keep watching instead of executing it. After dark the officers arrested two men in a truck loaded with nontaxpaid liquor. Jones's wife tried to block the doorway and demanded to see a warrant; the officers pushed past her, searched the house without a warrant (the daytime warrant having expired) and seized distilling equipment. Jones was arrested when he returned about an hour later.","procedure":"Jones moved to suppress the seized equipment. The district court denied the motion, finding probable cause for the search and relying on United States v. Rabinowitz, and convicted him after a bench trial. The Court of Appeals affirmed on the basis of the district judge's findings, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May federal officers search a dwelling and seize evidence without a warrant merely because they have probable cause to believe it contains contraband, or can the search be justified as incident to an arrest?","holding":"The search and seizure violated the Fourth Amendment. Probable cause alone cannot justify a warrantless search of a home, and the incident-to-arrest theory was not supported by the record because the officers entered to search, not to arrest.","reasoning":"The Court read the lower courts as upholding the search because the officers had probable cause to believe the house held contraband. That conflicts with the settled rule, drawn from Agnello and Taylor, that \"probable cause for belief that certain articles subject to seizure are in a dwelling cannot of itself justify a search without a warrant.\" Otherwise the Fourth Amendment's protection would be largely nullified, and Rule 41 on warrants, including the stricter rule for nighttime warrants, would make little sense. Rabinowitz did not apply because there the search depended on a valid arrest made first. The Government's new argument that the entry was to arrest Jones would raise a grave constitutional question about nighttime entry without an arrest warrant, but the Court did not reach it because the officers' testimony showed their purpose was \"to search for distilling equipment, and not to arrest petitioner.\" The entry could not be justified as disarming the boy with a shotgun, which was only a passing episode.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the result. Justice Clark, joined by Justice Burton, dissented. He argued that the Court wrongly diluted the lower courts' findings, which he read as treating probable cause as relating to arresting those in the house, and that officers with probable cause that a felon was inside could lawfully enter to arrest and then seize contraband found.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Black concurred in the result and Justices Clark and Burton dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep357/usrep357493/usrep357493.pdf"},"1955/72":{"facts":"Frank Costello was indicted for willfully attempting to evade income taxes for 1947-1949 by underreporting income. The only witnesses before the grand jury were three government agents who summarized financial evidence from transactions they had not witnessed firsthand. At trial the Government proved its net-worth case through 144 witnesses and 368 exhibits, and cross-examination revealed that the agents' hearsay was the only grand jury evidence.","procedure":"The trial court twice refused to dismiss the indictment, and Costello was convicted. The Court of Appeals affirmed (apart from one count reversed on other grounds), holding the indictment valid despite hearsay-only grand jury evidence. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on that single question.","question":"May a defendant be required to stand trial, and a conviction be sustained, where only hearsay evidence was presented to the grand jury that indicted him? More specifically, does the Fifth Amendment bar such an indictment, or should the Court, as a matter of supervisory power, allow challenges to indictments for inadequate or incompetent evidence?","holding":"An indictment returned by a legally constituted and unbiased grand jury, valid on its face, is enough to require trial on the merits, even if based solely on hearsay. The Fifth Amendment requires nothing more, and the Court declined to use its supervisory power to let defendants challenge indictments for inadequate or incompetent evidence.","reasoning":"The Court observed that \"neither the Fifth Amendment nor any other constitutional provision prescribes the kind of evidence upon which grand juries must act.\" It traced the grand jury to its English origins, where laymen acted free of rigid evidentiary rules and could act on their own knowledge. It relied on Holt v. United States, which refused to quash an indictment resting partly on incompetent evidence, and reasoned that permitting challenges to the sufficiency or competence of grand jury evidence would produce a preliminary trial and great delay. As to supervisory power, no persuasive reasons were offered for such a rule, which would run counter to the whole history of the grand jury. Defendants receive the full protection of the rules of evidence at trial on the merits.","separate":"Justice Burton concurred, agreeing with the judgment but objecting to the breadth of the Court's declarations. He would allow examination of grand jury action for bias or prejudice and would quash an indictment if the grand jury had no substantial or rationally persuasive evidence, but he found such evidence here and concurred for the reasons stated in the Court of Appeals' opinion (Judge Learned Hand).","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Black delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Clark and Harlan took no part. The text does not state a vote count beyond Justice Burton's concurrence in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep350/usrep350359/usrep350359.pdf"}});